# Copy to `.env` before bringing the stack up. Compose reads `.env` from this
# directory automatically, and refuses to start if any of the three settings
# below is missing or empty rather than starting something half-configured.
#
#   cp .env.example .env

# THE ONE VALUE THE AGENT AND ITS INGRESS SHARE. The agent admits it on
# /v1/storage; Caddy presents it there to keep its certificates, their keys and
# its ACME account in the bucket without ever holding a bucket credential.
#
# Any string will do here. A real one is generated, not typed:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Leaving it EMPTY is not the same as leaving it out: a node handed an empty
# token refuses to boot, because an empty token admits a caller presenting
# nothing. Compose refuses first, which is why this file exists.
SCAFO_STORAGE_TOKEN=playground-storage-token-change-me

# The bucket's credentials. Fake, local, and thrown away with the volume — the
# SeaweedFS in this stack accepts anything.
SCAFO_S3_ACCESS_KEY=scafo
SCAFO_S3_SECRET_KEY=scafo-secret

# The three ports this stack publishes on your machine. Left alone they are the
# ones the step sheet's commands use. Change one when something on this machine
# already has it — a `docker compose up` that hits a taken port fails with a
# message about the port and nothing about which service wanted it.
#
#   SCAFO_HTTP_PORT=80             the ingress, where applications answer
#   SCAFO_AGENT_PORT=7400          the agent API, where deploys are accepted
#   SCAFO_STORE_PORT=8333          the bucket, where the registry lives
#
# Every one of them is bound to 127.0.0.1 except the ingress, which is the only
# surface here meant to take traffic.

# Who the one-shot `cli` service runs as. It writes the operator key into this
# directory, which belongs to you and not to the image's uid 10001. If
# `docker compose run --rm cli …` fails with a permission error, put your own
# `id -u`:`id -g` here.
SCAFO_CLI_USER=1000:1000
